1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,378
A fan spinning at full speed.

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:04,549
A train pulling out of a station.

3
00:00:04,549 --> 00:00:06,290
A bicycle on a flat road.

4
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:08,083
All three are moving.

5
00:00:08,083 --> 00:00:11,998
But only one of them could be
moving in the simplest possible way.

6
00:00:12,448 --> 00:00:14,079
Aarav rides to college.

7
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:18,165
On the flat road, his bicycle
covers five metres every second.

8
00:00:18,515 --> 00:00:22,803
Then the road slopes down, and every
second he covers more than the second

9
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:23,703
before.

10
00:00:23,906 --> 00:00:25,136
Both are motion.

11
00:00:25,136 --> 00:00:28,587
So why does physics put
them in two different boxes?

12
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:29,717
Let's find out.

13
00:00:30,367 --> 00:00:32,081
First, uniform motion.

14
00:00:32,431 --> 00:00:37,240
An object is in uniform motion when
it covers equal displacements in equal

15
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:38,652
intervals of time.

16
00:00:39,052 --> 00:00:40,832
And here is the key part.

17
00:00:40,832 --> 00:00:43,378
Equal in every interval, however small.

18
00:00:43,378 --> 00:00:46,758
Every second, every half
second, every tiny instant.

19
00:00:47,358 --> 00:00:51,413
If the displacements are not
equal, the motion is non-uniform.

20
00:00:51,813 --> 00:00:54,977
Speeding up, slowing
down, or turning a corner.

21
00:00:54,977 --> 00:00:56,858
All of these are non-uniform.

22
00:00:57,508 --> 00:00:59,882
Let's test both on our motion bench.

23
00:00:59,882 --> 00:01:02,090
Two bicycles, one straight road.

24
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,489
Each bicycle drops a paint mark
on the road once every second.

25
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:10,526
The blue bicycle moves at a
steady five metres per second.

26
00:01:10,926 --> 00:01:14,799
The violet bicycle starts from
rest and speeds up the whole way.

27
00:01:15,199 --> 00:01:19,011
After eight seconds, both have
covered exactly forty metres.

28
00:01:19,011 --> 00:01:20,814
Same distance, same time.

29
00:01:21,314 --> 00:01:24,823
Let's replay it in slow motion
and look at the paint marks.

30
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:27,093
Blue marks: equal gaps.

31
00:01:27,093 --> 00:01:30,006
Five metres, five metres, five metres.

32
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:32,933
Violet marks: the gaps keep growing.

33
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:35,456
Its velocity arrow keeps getting longer.

34
00:01:36,106 --> 00:01:37,659
Now look at the graph.

35
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:39,447
Blue draws a straight line.

36
00:01:39,447 --> 00:01:41,913
Violet draws a curve that bends upward.

37
00:01:42,563 --> 00:01:44,853
Let's see why, one link at a time.

38
00:01:45,203 --> 00:01:46,156
Link one.

39
00:01:46,156 --> 00:01:50,571
Equal gaps in equal times means
the velocity never changes.

40
00:01:51,321 --> 00:01:52,243
Link two.

41
00:01:52,243 --> 00:01:56,097
Velocity never changes, so
the acceleration is zero.

42
00:01:56,847 --> 00:01:57,857
Link three.

43
00:01:57,857 --> 00:02:02,753
Constant velocity means position
grows by the same amount every second.

44
00:02:02,753 --> 00:02:04,970
x equals x nought plus v t.

45
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,620
Link four.

46
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:10,987
That is a straight line on the position
time graph, and its slope is the

47
00:02:10,987 --> 00:02:11,887
velocity.

48
00:02:12,462 --> 00:02:17,261
For non-uniform motion, the velocity
changes, so there is an acceleration,

49
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:18,847
and the graph curves.

50
00:02:19,547 --> 00:02:20,737
Remember this.

51
00:02:20,737 --> 00:02:25,108
Uniform motion means constant
velocity, and zero acceleration.

52
00:02:25,808 --> 00:02:27,070
Side by side.

53
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,320
Uniform.

54
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:29,303
Equal gaps.

55
00:02:29,303 --> 00:02:31,487
Straight position time line.

56
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:33,440
Flat velocity time line.

57
00:02:33,890 --> 00:02:34,862
Non-uniform.

58
00:02:34,862 --> 00:02:36,014
Growing gaps.

59
00:02:36,014 --> 00:02:38,749
A curve on the position time graph.

60
00:02:38,749 --> 00:02:41,009
A sloping velocity time line.

61
00:02:41,609 --> 00:02:42,712
Now you try.

62
00:02:42,712 --> 00:02:47,829
A train covers exactly ten kilometres
in every ten minutes, for one hour.

63
00:02:47,829 --> 00:02:49,970
Is its motion surely uniform?

64
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:51,340
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:55,498
Not surely!

66
00:02:55,498 --> 00:02:59,360
Inside each ten minutes it
could speed up and slow down.

67
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:03,775
Uniform needs equal displacements
in every interval, however small.

68
00:03:04,425 --> 00:03:05,325
One more.

69
00:03:05,245 --> 00:03:07,556
Here are two position time graphs.

70
00:03:07,956 --> 00:03:10,420
Graph A is a straight sloping line.

71
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:11,987
Graph B bends upward.

72
00:03:11,987 --> 00:03:14,125
Which one shows uniform motion?

73
00:03:14,125 --> 00:03:15,378
Pause and think.

74
00:03:18,578 --> 00:03:19,478
Graph A!

75
00:03:19,313 --> 00:03:23,970
A straight line has the same slope
everywhere, so the velocity is constant.

76
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:26,871
Graph B gets steeper,
so it is speeding up.

77
00:03:27,521 --> 00:03:29,257
Now, one worked example.

78
00:03:29,607 --> 00:03:34,258
Aarav rides six hundred metres at five
metres per second, then six hundred

79
00:03:34,258 --> 00:03:36,578
metres at three metres per second.

80
00:03:36,578 --> 00:03:38,436
What is his average speed?

81
00:03:38,936 --> 00:03:42,126
The first part takes six
hundred divided by five.

82
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:44,705
That is one hundred and twenty seconds.

83
00:03:45,455 --> 00:03:48,653
The second part takes six
hundred divided by three.

84
00:03:48,653 --> 00:03:50,521
That is two hundred seconds.

85
00:03:51,271 --> 00:03:54,575
Average speed is total
distance over total time.

86
00:03:54,575 --> 00:03:58,470
Twelve hundred metres in three
hundred and twenty seconds.

87
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:02,343
That is three point seven
five metres per second.

88
00:04:02,343 --> 00:04:03,243
Not four!

89
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:05,764
He spends more time at the slower speed.

90
00:04:06,414 --> 00:04:07,880
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:13,218
Many students say a body moving in a
circle at constant speed is in uniform

92
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:14,118
motion.

93
00:04:14,269 --> 00:04:18,870
But its direction changes every
instant, so its velocity changes.

94
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:21,718
That is accelerated, non-uniform motion.

95
00:04:21,718 --> 00:04:24,578
Constant speed is not constant velocity.

96
00:04:25,228 --> 00:04:26,789
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:26,789 --> 00:04:31,567
As concept questions: reading position
time and velocity time graphs, and

98
00:04:31,567 --> 00:04:34,689
finding average speed
for a journey in parts.

99
00:04:35,289 --> 00:04:36,530
Exam level now.

100
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:41,248
A particle moves along a line with
x equals two t squared, minus eight

101
00:04:41,248 --> 00:04:42,488
t, plus five.

102
00:04:42,488 --> 00:04:44,422
Which statement is correct?

103
00:04:44,422 --> 00:04:45,715
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:51,115 --> 00:04:52,490
The answer is B.

105
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:55,330
Velocity is four t minus eight.

106
00:04:55,330 --> 00:04:59,632
It is zero at t equals two
seconds, and it keeps changing.

107
00:04:59,632 --> 00:05:01,745
So the motion is non-uniform.

108
00:05:02,245 --> 00:05:05,211
A is wrong, the velocity is not constant.

109
00:05:05,211 --> 00:05:08,538
C is wrong, the acceleration
is four everywhere.

110
00:05:08,538 --> 00:05:09,548
D is wrong.

111
00:05:09,548 --> 00:05:14,533
At four seconds, x is back to five
metres, so the displacement is zero.

112
00:05:14,533 --> 00:05:16,696
Sixteen metres is the distance.

113
00:05:17,296 --> 00:05:18,758
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:18,758 --> 00:05:20,261
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:26,298
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:26,698 --> 00:05:27,598
One.

117
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:32,008
Why is equal distance every minute
not enough to call a motion uniform?

118
00:05:32,908 --> 00:05:33,808
Two.

119
00:05:33,385 --> 00:05:37,519
What does a straight position time
graph tell you about the velocity?

120
00:05:38,419 --> 00:05:39,319
Three.

121
00:05:39,076 --> 00:05:43,789
Why is a fan blade turning at
constant speed not in uniform motion?

122
00:05:44,889 --> 00:05:49,764
Next time: if the velocity changes
by the same amount every second, can

123
00:05:49,764 --> 00:05:52,553
we predict exactly where Aarav will be?

124
00:05:52,553 --> 00:05:54,245
Three equations do it.

125
00:05:54,245 --> 00:05:57,065
Equations of uniformly accelerated motion.
